"content":"VIDEO QII.1 in Action 3 min watch Infrastructure as the Backbone of Jobs and Livelihoods Infrastructure can create a virtuous economic cycle, from job creation during the construction and O&M phases, to boosting the industries that support these activities, to ultimately increasing productivity, jobs and access to markets through the infrastructure services provided. 1 1: World Bank (2020) Jobs Umbrella MDTF: Annual Report 2019-2020 Co-Benefits of Integrating QII Principles Beyond its role as an economic driver, infrastructure must align with environmental and social objectives. While each QII principle stands on its own, governments should consider how multiple principles can be combined within a single project. The result is co-benefits that no single approach can achieve alone.1 1: Vagliasindi and Gorgulu (2025). Disentangling the Key Economic Channels through Which Infrastructure Affects Jobs Digitalization and Technology in Infrastructure Digitizing infrastructure services and introducing innovative technologies are cross-cutting interventions that amplify QII impacts. They enable step-change benefits in governance and planning processes, enhance designs and operational efficiency, reduce whole of life costs, and attract skilled labor to the sector. This is about “making infrastructure smarter and more connected”, not only about digital infrastructure itself. Partnering with the Private Sector Investment needs for physical infrastructure alone require around 4.5% of GDP, or $1.5 trillion annually, in developing countries. For many nations, this represents a significant hurdle. Bridging this financing gap while managing rising debt levels and budgetary constraints requires bringing together public and private capital.1 1: World Bank (2025) Foundations for Growth and Jobs  "
